eryone wants more of it  well that means we end up paying higher  prices so yes environmental policy might  play a part in higher global gas prices  but it's only one of lots of factors but  there's also something you've probably  noticed because it's there in your  monthly energy bill this is the  breakdown of a typical bill as of the  latest off gem numbers that that price  cap coming in and have a look i'm going  to break it down and you can see the  constituent part wholesale costs by far  the biggest part in other words that's  your international gas prices policy  costs over here  there you go now that includes some  environmental contributions but they're  not enormous but they're not trivial  either about eight percent  of bills uh and they do make our bills  higher but here's the thing that is  largely a consequence of the past here's  why renewables used to be much more  expensive than other forms of power this  is one way of looking at it this is  called levelized cost and it's looking  at the energy costs over the entire  lifetime of the plant for things like  gas coal wind and solar this was back in  2009 but just look at what's happened  since then this is 2021 isn't that  extraordinary i'm going to show you just  one more time so back in 2009 here's how  much more expensive wind and solar were  and just look at what's happened since  then they've come down enormously now  cheaper than  gas or coal power stations it's pretty  extraordinary isn't it but we're still  paying off the bill for those expensive  years when those bars were really high  but it's worth noting something else  something important these bars are the  average cost over 30 years so with  fossil fuels that's mostly well that's  fuel it's gas  and coal for the boilers with renewables  the wind and the solar power are free  but most of the cost comes up front so  let's look rather than looking at that  spread over a period of decades let's  look just at those upfront costs so same  kind